.TH TIMBERSH 1 "2026-08-30" "timberfs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timbersh \- an interactive shell for timberfs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timbersh
.RI [ --cmd " ARGV" ]
.RI [ --hosts " H,H" ]
.RI [ --rc " FILE" ]
.RI [ --ttl " SECS" ]
.RI [ --page " N" ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A console for reading timberfs stores, in a grammar borrowed from SQL. It
selects the stores to read with a predicate, searches them, and pages
through the answer.
.PP
⚠
.B EXPERIMENTAL.
The statements it accepts are NOT promised and change without notice. It
exists to be USED \(em a protocol nobody writes a client against is one
whose awkward parts stay theoretical, and every awkward part this has
found became a fix in timberfs. Do not build anything on its grammar.
.PP
It speaks
.BR timberfs\-query\-document (5)
and nothing else: every statement becomes a document handed to a timberfs
on standard input, and every answer is read back the same way. It
implements no part of the store SELECTOR \(em each host resolves its own,
so a predicate means here exactly what it means there.
.SH WHERE THE FLEET IS
A
.B target
is a name and the way to reach it: an argv to run, or a URL to POST the
document to. The transport belongs to the TARGET rather than to the
session, so an
.BR ssh (1)
to one host, a site wrapper taking the host as an argument, and an HTTP
endpoint on a unix socket are one fleet \(em which one command with a
placeholder could not express.
.PP
The document a resolver prints, and the file that holds the same thing:
.PP
.RS
.EX
{"v": "1.0-EXPERIMENTAL",
 "refresh": {"cmd": ["fleet-sweep", "--cached"]},
 "targets": [
   {"name": "mail01", "cmd": ["ssh", "mail01", "timberfs", "query", "--query", "-"]},
   {"name": "web01",  "cmd": ["site-wrapper", "query", "web01"]},
   {"name": "db01",   "url": "http://db01:9099/query"},
   {"name": "local",  "url": "unix+http:///run/timberfs.sock//query"},
   {"cmd": ["timberfs", "query", "--query", "-"]}
 ]}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B cmd
is a LIST because a command line written as one string would have to be
split again at this end, under rules that are ours to invent and get
wrong.
.PP
A
.B url
is POSTed the document and answers with the stream, read as it arrives
exactly as a pipe is. A unix socket is written into it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
http://db01:9099/query                      https:// too
unix+http:///run/timberfs.sock//query       dial it, POST /query
unix+http:///run/timberfs.sock              the request path defaults to /
unix+http://logs.internal/run/x.sock//q     and Host: logs.internal
.EE
.RE
.PP
⚠
.B //
is the boundary between the socket and the request path, because it is
the one sequence that cannot MEAN anything inside a filesystem path
\(em POSIX collapses repeated slashes, so a
.B //
in a path names the file a single
.B /
does. Every other candidate (:, #, !) can legally be part of a socket
path, and one holding the separator would split in the wrong place
SILENTLY. The first one decides; later ones stay in the request path,
where HTTP allows them. The socket path is then percent-decoded, being a
filesystem path rather than a wire path.
.PP
⚠ The boundary is NOT found by statting the prefixes. That would make a
document stop parsing when the agent is down, so \(lqthis target is
unreachable\(rq and \(lqthis URL is malformed\(rq would be one error, and no
document could be reviewed or tested without first creating its sockets.
.PP
⚠ NOT
.BR http+unix:// ,
which is requests-unixsocket\(aqs and spells the socket percent-encoded in
the AUTHORITY. That grammar needs no boundary, but it spends the
authority on the socket and so has nowhere to put a
.BR Host: .
Writing one gets a message naming the other.
.PP
The host is OPTIONAL and is never dialled \(em the socket is. It is the
.B Host:
header, which is what a server routing several names off one socket
reads; absent, it is
.BR localhost .
.PP
A target is reached ONE way: a
.B cmd
and a
.B url
on the same target is a mistake in the document and is named, not
resolved by preferring one. A member this build does not read leaves that
target NAMED rather than reached anyway \(em it may be the member that
says how.
.PP
⚠ A url target has NO stderr. timberfs writes the sentences that explain
an answer which looks wrong \(em
.BR "no store matches \(rs..." ,
.B "retention overtook this follower"
\(em to fd 2 and still exits 0, and HTTP has no second channel to carry
them on. A url target's notes therefore arrive only when the request
FAILS, out of the response body. Closing that gap means deciding what a
timberfs server puts on the wire, which is deliberately not decided yet.
A
.B name
is what the
.B HOST
column and a
.BI timber:// host /
address say; it is a HINT and not identity, so renaming a target, or
changing how it is reached, leaves every written address valid.
.PP
Sources, most explicit first \(em
.B show hosts;
says which won:
.BR \-\-resolver ,
.B \-\-targets
or
.BR \-\-cmd / \-\-hosts
(one of them, not two);
.BR $TIMBERFS_RESOLVER ;
.BR $TIMBERFS_TARGETS ;
.BR $TIMBERFS_CMD / $TIMBERFS_HOSTS ;
.IR ~/.config/timberfs/targets.json ,
.IR /etc/timberfs/targets.json ;
then one local
.B timberfs query --query -
found on
.BR PATH .
.PP
⚠ A resolver that failed is FATAL and an empty fleet is REFUSED. Falling
back would answer a question about one fleet with a different one, and a
session that quietly asks the local machine instead looks exactly like a
fleet that held nothing.
.PP
⚠ A target that FAILED is not fatal. Something in the system being down
must not stop you reading the logs that are available, so a target that
does not answer is named and the rest are still asked. The two failures
differ: the resolver is how you know what the fleet IS, and being wrong
about that makes every later answer describe the wrong thing; one machine
refusing a connection only means its logs are missing from this answer.
.PP
⚠ A target this build cannot reach \(em a
.B url
whose scheme it cannot dial, a future member in place of both \(em is
NAMED, never dropped, for the same reason: a host that was never asked
must not read as a host that had nothing.
.PP
The resolver is asked ONE question, what the fleet is, and gets no
arguments.
.B refresh
asks again, because a resolver derives its answer \(em by default the way
the fleet was produced, and otherwise as named by
.BR \-\-refresh ,
.BR $TIMBERFS_REFRESH ,
or the document's own
.BR refresh ,
in that order. Getting the fleet and re-asking for it may cost different
amounts \(em a full sweep to open a session with, a cheap one to re-ask
\(em so they are allowed to be different. A document's
.B refresh
applies to the fleet it arrived with: a refresh whose document names none
of its own leaves the next one re-running whatever produced the fleet
originally.
.PP
A RESOLVER is a command or a url, and
.B \-\-refresh
is too. A flag and an export are one string either way, so a url is told
from a command by its SCHEME \(em a command is a program name or a path,
and neither can carry one:
.PP
.RS
.EX
TIMBERFS_RESOLVER='fleet-sweep --json'
TIMBERFS_RESOLVER=https://fleet.internal/timberfs/targets
TIMBERFS_RESOLVER=unix+http:///run/timberfs-agent.sock//fleet
.EE
.RE
.PP
A resolver is asked ONE question and gets no arguments, so a url one is a
GET \(em where a target, which is handed a document, is a POST. A
resolver that failed is fatal whichever it is; a non-2xx carries the body
as the reason, being the only channel a url has. Something spelled like a
url whose scheme this build cannot dial is a url with a TYPO, and says
so, rather than being run as a program.
.PP
The document's
.B refresh
says which it is rather than being sniffed, JSON having somewhere to put
that:
.B {"cmd": [...]}
or
.BR {"url":\ "..."} ,
the same two words a target is reached by.
.PP
⚠
.B \-\-targets
stays a FILE. A url DERIVES its answer, which is what makes
.B refresh
worth asking again; a file is a thing you edit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-resolver " CMD|URL"
A command that prints the fleet as a target document, or a url to GET it
from \(em told apart by the url's scheme.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_RESOLVER )
.TP
.BI \-\-targets " FILE"
The same document, from a file.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_TARGETS )
.TP
.BI \-\-cmd " ARGV"
One command reaching every host, with
.B _TIMBERHOST_
substituted per
.BR \-\-hosts .
Default
.BR "timberfs query --query -" .
It is handed the document on stdin, so anything that reaches a timberfs
works \(em a wrapper,
.BR ssh (1),
a container exec.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_CMD )
.TP
.BI \-\-refresh " CMD|URL"
How
.B refresh
re-derives the fleet, where that is not how it was produced. It
is not a fourth way to say where the fleet is, so it does not join the
one-of-three rule above.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_REFRESH ;
then the document\(aqs
.BR refresh ;
then whatever produced the fleet)
.TP
.BI \-\-hosts " H,H"
The hosts that command reaches. Their stores present as one set with a
.B HOST
column, each remembering where it lives.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_HOSTS )
.RS
.PP
A read goes to every host at once and is rendered AS IT ARRIVES, each line
naming the host it came from \(em so a slow host does not hold up a fast
one. Nothing is lost by that: within a host a bounded answer is
.B order=sequential
(store after store, not time \(em see
.BR timberfs\-records (5))
and across hosts no order was ever claimed. A
.B limit
is a total, so which entries a bounded fleet read returns is decided by
arrival; it says how many more had already come.
.PP
Several hosts with no
.B _TIMBERHOST_
in
.B \-\-cmd
is refused: every host would get the same command, so one forest would be
read N times and every entry returned N times under a different host's
name.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-rc " FILE"
Statements run at startup, default
.IR ~/.timbershrc .
It is RUN, not read \(em a script of statements, the way
.I .psqlrc
is.
.RB ( $TIMBERSH_RC )
.TP
.BI \-\-histfile " FILE"
Where the line history is kept, default
.IR ~/.timbersh\-history .
.RB ( $TIMBERSH_HISTFILE )
.RS
.PP
⚠ Created mode 0600, and created that way rather than widened and then
narrowed \(em between those two calls the file is readable, which is the
window the mode exists to close. A history of queries is a list of the
identifiers somebody went looking for: request ids, customer names,
hostnames. That is production data in a dotfile, and
.BR psql (1)
has the same exposure and the same answer.
.PP
Failing to read or write it never stops a session. A read\-only home, a
missing directory, a file owned by somebody else \(em none of that is a
reason to refuse to open a shell, so it is reported once and the session
runs without history.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-histsize " N"
How many lines to keep, default 2000.
.RB ( $TIMBERSH_HISTSIZE )
.TP
.BI \-\-ttl " SECS"
Expire the cached store list after SECS. Default 0, which never expires
it \(em
.B refresh
re-reads it and says what changed.
.RB ( $TIMBERSH_STORE_TTL )
.TP
.BI \-\-page " N"
Entries per CALL on an unbounded read, default 10000. Not the
statement's
.BR limit :
that says how much of the ANSWER you want, this says how much of it may
be in memory at once. Nothing streams, so an unbounded read otherwise
buffers the whole answer \(em times the hosts asked in parallel.
.RB ( $TIMBERSH_PAGE )
.RS
.PP
An unbounded
.B records
read is walked a page at a time, handing the
.B position
records back as the next page's
.BR cursor .
A read with a
.B limit
is ONE call: that bound is a total enforced here, so a later host's
entries are read and dropped, and a page built from its positions would
resume past exactly what nobody saw.
.B loglines
and
.B chunks
carry no
.B position
record, so they are left unpaged rather than truncated.
.RE
.TP
.B \-q
Start without printing the help.
.SH HOSTS AT RUNTIME
.B "add host NAME"
asks it from now on;
.B "drop host NAME"
stops. Both take several names, and both re-read the store list at once,
because the cached one is tagged with the host each store came from and
would otherwise answer for a fleet that is no longer the one being asked.
.PP
A host set and a command must agree, and the disagreement is refused in
BOTH directions because each is wrong in the same silent way. Several
hosts with no
.B _TIMBERHOST_
to put them in sends the same command to each, so one forest is read N
times and every entry comes back N times under a different host's name. A
placeholder with no host to fill it runs the command with
.B _TIMBERHOST_
still in it \(em which reaches something, or nothing, and reports
whatever that was.
.PP
Dropping the last host is refused: a shell with none has nothing to ask.
.SH THE INVESTIGATION'S WINDOW
.B "create session from '13:00' to '14:00';"
states the time frame of what you are looking at, once, instead of on
every statement \(em which is how it comes to be forgotten on one.
.B "show session;"
and
.B "drop session;"
are the rest of it; either end may be left out.
.PP
Every statement is then read INSIDE it. A
.B "where logline since ..."
may narrow within the session and never out of it; one that reaches past
is answered inside and says so, and one with no overlap at all is refused
rather than answered emptily \(em an empty answer and a question that
could not have one look the same.
.PP
It bounds the PAGER too, which is the half a statement cannot:
.B "into view"
and
.B o
open a tape, and a tape has no predicate to keep it near the incident. It
stops at the window instead, and
.B t
changes it from in there \(em see
.BR timberview (1).
.PP
⚠ Applied on the WRITE clock, widened, wherever the answer is addressed
by it \(em a
.B chunks
read and the tape. A chunk written after the window can hold entries
stamped inside it, so the bound can only ever over-include, never lose a
line that belongs.
.SH STATEMENTS
.B \(rsd
lists the stores;
.B \(rsd NAME
or
.B \(rsd ID
describes one.
.B show hosts;
says what each host is and whether anything it said went wrong.
.B \(rs?
prints the grammar, which is the authority \(em this page deliberately
does not copy it, because it moves.
.PP
.B view
opens a store as a tape rather than as an answer, IN PROCESS: the loop it
exists for is search, jump, search again, and a separate program would
have to leave the screen on every hop. It is the same viewer
.BR timberview (1)
runs alone, and it leaves you the address of where you stopped.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timberview (1),
.BR timberfs (1),
.BR timberfs\-query\-document (5),
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
